<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-04">
  <title>Week 4: Partial derivatives; higher-order derivatives</title>

<introduction>

<p>
In single-variable calculus we studied functions whose inputs are real
numbers. In this chapter we investigate functions whose inputs are points
in the plane. Such functions may represent elevation, temperature,
pressure, population density, or cost over a geographic region.
</p>

<p>
Many ideas from Calculus I reappear in this richer setting:
limits, derivatives, tangent lines, maxima, and minima. At the same time,
new phenomena emerge because points in the plane can move in infinitely
many directions.
</p>

</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-limits">

<title>Limits and Continuity</title>

<p>
In one-variable calculus a point can approach a target value from the
left or from the right. In the plane, however, a point may approach a
target along infinitely many paths.
</p>

<image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[below right] at (1,0) {$(a,b)$};

\draw[thick,blue]
(-2,0)--(0,0);

\draw[thick,red]
(-2,-1)..controls(-1,-.5)..(0,0);

\draw[thick,green!60!black]
(0,2)..controls(.5,1)..(0,0);

\draw[thick,purple]
(2,1)..controls(1,.2)..(0,0);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<investigation>

<p>
Why does checking only one or two paths toward a point fail to prove that
a limit exists?
</p>

</investigation>

<definition xml:id="def-limit-2d">

<p>
Let <m>f</m> be defined near the point <m>(a,b)</m>.
We say

<me>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)}f(x,y)=L
</me>

when the values of <m>f(x,y)</m> become arbitrarily close to
<m>L</m> whenever <m>(x,y)</m> is sufficiently close to
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>

</definition>

<definition>

<p>
A function <m>f</m> is <term>continuous</term> at
<m>(a,b)</m> if

<me>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)}f(x,y)=f(a,b).
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<remark>

<p>
Most functions encountered in calculus, including polynomials,
trigonometric functions, exponentials, and rational functions where
defined, are continuous on their domains.
</p>

</remark>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-partials">

<title>Partial Derivatives</title>

<p>
For a function of two variables we may ask how the output changes when
only one input changes. This leads to the notion of a partial derivative.
</p>

<subsubsection>

<title>Coordinate Traces</title>

<p>
Fixing one variable produces a curve obtained from the surface.
The slope of this curve determines a partial derivative.
</p>

<image width="70%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[fill=gray!20] plot[smooth]
coordinates{
(-4.3,.6)
(-3,1.2)
(-2,2.4)
(-1,1.8)
(-0.4,1.1) (-2,0.5) (-3,0)
(-4,0.3)}--cycle;
\draw[dashed] plot[smooth] coordinates{(-4.3,0.6)
(-2,1.2) (-0.4,1.1)};
\draw[->] (-0.2,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.2)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$z$};

\draw[thick,blue]
plot[smooth]
coordinates{
(.3,.6)
(1,1.2)
(2,2.4)
(3,1.8)
(3.6,1.1)
};

\draw[dashed]
(2,0)--(2,2.4);

\node at (3,2.7) {Plane $x=c$};
\node at (-3,2.7) {Surface};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</subsubsection>

<definition xml:id="def-partials">

<p>

The partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m> is

<me>
f_x(x,y)=
\lim_{h\to0}
\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}.
</me>

The partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>y</m> is

<me>
f_y(x,y)=
\lim_{h\to0}
\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}.
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<remark>

<p>
When computing <m>f_x</m>, treat <m>y</m> as a constant.
When computing <m>f_y</m>, treat <m>x</m> as a constant.
</p>

</remark>

<example>

<statement>

Find the first partial derivatives of

<me>
f(x,y)=x^3-2x+y^2-5.
</me>

</statement>

<solution>

<me>
f_x=3x^2-2
</me>

and

<me>
f_y=2y.
</me>

</solution>

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-directional">

<title>Directional Derivatives and the Gradient</title>

<p>
Partial derivatives measure rates of change in the coordinate directions.
What if we move in an arbitrary direction?
</p>

<subsubsection>

<title>A Hiking Interpretation</title>


<image width="60%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw (0,0) ellipse (3 and 2);
\draw (0.5,-0.1) ellipse (2.1 and 1.4);
\draw (1,-0.1) ellipse (1.2 and .8);

\draw[fill=white,white] (2.65,-0.2) rectangle (3.5,0);
\draw[fill=white,white] (2.25,-0.55) rectangle (2.7,-.25);
\draw[fill=white,white] (1.5,-0.8) rectangle (2.2,-.6);
\node[right] at (0.8,.3) {$P$};
\node[right] at (2.6,-0.1) {\tiny $z=15$};
\node[right] at (2,-0.45) {\tiny $z=10$};
\node[right] at (1.4,-0.7) {\tiny $z=5$};
\draw[thick,blue,->]
(1.7,0)--(2.5,0);

\draw[thick,red,->]
(1.7,0)--(1.7,1.3);
\fill (1.7,0) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<investigation>

<ol>

<li>Which hiker appears to gain elevation faster?</li>

<li>Which direction seems closest to perpendicular to the contour lines?</li>

<li>Which direction should produce the greatest rate of increase?</li>

</ol>

</investigation>

</subsubsection>

<definition>

<p>

Let <m>\mathbf u</m> be a unit vector.

The <term>directional derivative</term> of <m>f</m>
in the direction <m>\mathbf u</m> is

<me>
D_{\mathbf u}f
=
\nabla f \cdot \mathbf u.
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<definition>

<p>

The <term>gradient</term> of <m>f</m> is

<me>
\nabla f
=
\left\langle
f_x,
f_y
\right\rangle.
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<theorem>

<p>

The gradient points in the direction of greatest increase of a function.

</p>

</theorem>

<theorem>

<p>

The gradient is perpendicular to level curves.

</p>

</theorem>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-second-partials">

<title>Higher-Order Partial Derivatives</title>

<definition>

<p>

Second-order partial derivatives include

<me>
f_{xx},
\qquad
f_{yy},
\qquad
f_{xy},
\qquad
f_{yx}.
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<example>

<statement>

Verify Clairaut's theorem for

<me>
f(x,y)
=
x^2y^3+x\sin(y).
</me>

</statement>

<solution>

<me>
f_x
=
2xy^3+\sin(y)
</me>

and therefore

<me>
f_{xy}
=
6xy^2+\cos(y).
</me>

Also,

<me>
f_y
=
3x^2y^2+x\cos(y)
</me>

so

<me>
f_{yx}
=
6xy^2+\cos(y).
</me>

Consequently,

<me>
f_{xy}=f_{yx}.
</me>

</solution>

</example>

<theorem xml:id="thm-clairaut">

<p>

If the mixed partial derivatives are continuous on an open region, then

<me>
f_{xy}=f_{yx}.
</me>

</p>

</theorem>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-optimization">

<title>Optimization</title>

<p>
Optimization in several variables extends the ideas of local maxima and
local minima from Calculus I.
</p>

<subsubsection>

<title>From One Dimension to Two Dimensions</title>

