</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>

<title>Saddle Points</title>

<p>
Two-dimensional functions exhibit a behavior that has no analogue in one
dimension.
</p>

<image width="55%">
<latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[line width=1pt,fill=gray!20]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates {(-4,4) (-2,1) (0,0) (2,1)
               (4,4)(4,3)(3,0)(2,-2)(1,-2.9)(0,-3.1)(-1,-2.9)(-2,-2)(-3,0)(-4,3)(-4,4)};

\draw[line width=1pt]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates {(-13,4) (-11,1) (-9,0) (-7,1)
               (-5,4)};
        \draw[->,red,line width=1pt] (-3.7,3.5) arc (-30:218:1);             
               \draw[line width=1pt,dashed]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates{(0,0)(-0.5,-0.5)(-1,-2.5)};\draw[line width=1pt,dashed]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates {(4,4)(3,1)(2,-1.2)(1,-2.5)(0,-2.7)(-1,-2.5)(-2,-1.2)(-3,1)(-4,4)};
                 \draw[line width=1pt]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates{(0,0)(0.5,-0.5)(1,-2.9)};
  \draw[line width=1pt]
  plot[smooth]
  coordinates {(8,-2.9)(7.5,-0.5)(7,0)(6.5,-0.5)(6,-2.9)};
\fill[blue] (0,0) circle (7pt);
        \draw[->,red,line width=1pt] (1,-2.9) arc (250:300:6);      
\fill[blue] (7,0) circle (7pt);
\fill[blue] (-9,0) circle (7pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<caption>
A saddle point increases in some directions and decreases in others.
</caption>


</subsubsection>

<definition>

<p>

A point is a <term>critical point</term> if

<me>
f_x=0
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
f_y=0,
</me>

or one or both partial derivatives fail to exist.

</p>

</definition>

<definition>

<p>

For a critical point,

<me>
D
=
f_{xx}f_{yy}
-
(f_{xy})^2.
</me>

</p>

</definition>

<theorem>

<p>

Let <m>P</m> be a critical point.

</p>

<ul>

<li>
If <m>D>0</m> and <m>f_{xx}>0</m>,
then <m>P</m> is a local minimum.
</li>

<li>
If <m>D>0</m> and <m>f_{xx}&lt; 0</m>,
then <m>P</m> is a local maximum.
</li>

<li>
If <m>D&lt;0</m>,
then <m>P</m> is a saddle point.
</li>

<li>
If <m>D=0</m>,
the test is inconclusive.
</li>

</ul>

</theorem>

<investigation>

<p>

Examine a contour map containing several marked points.

Without computing derivatives, determine which points appear to be

</p>

<ul>

<li>local maxima,</li>
<li>local minima,</li>
<li>saddle points,</li>
<li>ordinary points.</li>
</ul>

</investigation>

</subsection>

</section>
